vattery 0.7.9 - EasyOS edition
==============================

Credits
-------
vattery was written by Jezra J Lickter, (c) 2011
(http://www.jezra.net/projects/vattery). See AUTHORS, README and COPYING.
This edition only adds changes for EasyOS on top of his program; they are
listed below and in ChangeLog. Licence: GNU GPL v3 or later, same as the
original.

A battery monitor for the JWM tray in Barry Kauler's EasyOS (tested to build
against GTK 2.24; EasyOS "Excalibur" uses X11 and the JWM tray, which
vattery needs). Mouse over the icon to see the percentage, right-click it for
Preferences, About and Quit.

Install
-------
1. Compiling needs gcc. On EasyOS, click the "devx" container icon on the
   desktop, then in a terminal:

     tar xzf vattery-0.7.5-easyos.tar.gz
     cd vattery-0.7.5-easyos
     ./build-easyos.sh

   If it says GTK2 development files are missing: install libgtk2.0-dev

2. On the main desktop, from the same folder:

     ./build-easyos.sh
     
   (If you built inside the devx container, copy the folder to /files first
   so the main desktop can see it.)

  Files in this package
---------------------
  AUTHORS, COPYING, README   original author, licence and readme (unchanged)
  ChangeLog                  version history, original and EasyOS
  README-EASYOS              this file
  build-easyos.sh            builds, installs and uninstalls vattery
  src/*.c                    the C code that build-easyos.sh compiles
  vala/*.vala, config.vapi   the original Vala source, with the EasyOS changes
  easyos/                    tray start-up script, menu entry, alert helper

You only need the vala/ folder if you want to change the program. After
editing a .vala file, turn it into C again (needs the Vala compiler):

  cd vala
  valac -C --vapidir=. --pkg config --pkg gtk+-2.0 \
        main.vala configuration.vala configurationGUI.vala battery.vala conservation.vala
  mv *.c ../src/
  cd .. && ./build-easyos.sh

The old autotools build files (configure, Makefiles...) and the unused
translation files were removed; build-easyos.sh replaces them.

What changed from 0.7.5
-----------------------
* Finds batteries by their "type" in /sys/class/power_supply, not only BAT*
  names, and ignores mouse/keyboard batteries.
* Reads energy_*, charge_* or capacity files, so newer laptops work.
* "Full", "Not charging" and "Unknown" while plugged in now show the charged
  colour instead of the draining colour.
* Low-battery alerts use vattery-alert, which picks gxmessage, yad, Xdialog,
  notify-send or xmessage - whichever EasyOS has. You can still type your own
  command in Preferences.
* Shows 0% correctly instead of "no batteries found"; no crash when a battery
  reports a capacity of 0.
* Added Quit to the tray menu.
* C sources regenerated with Vala 0.56, so no Vala compiler is needed.

Settings are kept in /root/.config/vattery/config as before.

Fix in v2 (2026-11-21)
----------------------
The tray icon could show an empty white battery with no blue/green/red bar
when the system language uses a decimal comma (Finnish, German, Swedish...).
The bar's size was written as "57,0", which the SVG reader rejects. vattery
now writes whole numbers, so the bar shows in every language. Text mode's
"%" sign is fixed too.

Fix in v3 (2026-11-21)
----------------------
- Commands now run through the shell (/bin/sh -c), so things like
  "echo mem > /sys/power/state" (suspend), pipes and && work.
- After you press Save in Preferences, vattery checks the new alert levels
  straight away instead of waiting for the battery level to change.
- Every command vattery runs is printed, e.g. "vattery: running: ...".
  Start vattery from a terminal to see them.
Alerts only fire while running on battery, not while the charger is in.

New in v4 (2026-11-22)
----------------------
- Hovering over the tray icon now shows the remaining time, e.g.
    63 % - 6 hours 5 mins remaining time     (on battery)
    80 % - 30 mins until full                (charging)
    100 % - fully charged
  It is worked out from the battery's power draw (power_now or current_now in
  /sys/class/power_supply), smoothed so it doesn't jump around. Just after
  plugging in or unplugging, give it a few seconds to settle. If the battery
  doesn't report its power draw, only the percentage is shown.
- The tray text is also printed in the terminal ("vattery: tray: ...")
  whenever it changes.
- Your settings (warning/critical commands etc.) are kept as they are.

